c849414d51.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph by a factor of $2.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $(0,2),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/b7/b747ae8c29f01b840930d81f9c4b706d049ca685ee982efff717ddb13213937d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059865 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8e49a0c-9575-5bd0-af4e-6463495e56b3" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Sum of Functions With Constant Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5446db9d-f32b-503a-98c3-71bd4b13a940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9{x}^{1/4}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285562} {:answer/id #uuid "72b849ec-c73e-51c6-8bd6-7d57955bf8d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{4\\sqrt[3]{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285843} {:answer/id #uuid "93d4bb13-717f-50aa-afa5-f6147b1c9f75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}-9{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286135} {:answer/id #uuid "a7da2020-89e3-5d5f-bbdb-6fb669e629c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{9{x}^{-1/4}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286320} {:answer/id #uuid "e9029ff2-9352-53d1-a4ea-86df802fa96d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{4\\sqrt[4]{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9029ff2-9352-53d1-a4ea-86df802fa96d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{4\\sqrt[4]{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286919} :answer-field/id #uuid "8320dfd3-7a24-5485-a9b9-30a0c49ac165" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "76292ca7-955e-5da2-801e-cf1602a2e16d" :question/math-academy-id "q-1856" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-3{x}^{3/4}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply the addition, constant multiple, and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-3{x}^{3/4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{3/4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-2})-3\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-2){x}^{(-2-1)}-3\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}{x}^{(3/4-1)} \\\\  & =-4{x}^{-3}-\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{-1/4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{4\\sqrt[4]{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100892 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8de0dd63-23c6-5926-9e33-71e517949af2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficient of a Particular Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46d28906-8490-5c29-b464-0d5c1a48004e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,096" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285441} {:answer/id #uuid "99a94d50-1eb7-5245-b930-e867aafb297a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,640" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286196} {:answer/id #uuid "9b9888fc-dc7e-5ae3-99b6-a76b342aa777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,480" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286217} {:answer/id #uuid "cba56c64-acf9-5648-abc7-affc33124f5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,560" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286645} {:answer/id #uuid "d952dfee-02a8-55ff-a0dc-3cc35185c0a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,868" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99a94d50-1eb7-5245-b930-e867aafb297a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,640" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286196} :answer-field/id #uuid "90078a64-d272-59e6-bb4a-64d828c75a76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fe2343c-e168-571c-8f87-3172e7d9973d" :question/math-academy-id "q-18624" :question/problem "The coefficient of ${x}^{12}$ in the expansion of ${(2+x)}^{15}$ is" :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(2+x)}^{15},$ we have $a=2,$ $b=x,$ and $n=15.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2+x)^{15}=(\\frac{15}{0}){2}^{15}{x}^{0}+(\\frac{15}{1}){2}^{15-1}{x}^{1}+(\\frac{15}{2}){2}^{15-2}{x}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{15}{15}){2}^{15-15}{x}^{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the ${x}^{12}$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\frac{15}{12}){2}^{15-12}{x}^{12}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the coefficient, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{15}{12}){2}^{15-12} & =455\\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =455\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =3\\,640.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{12}$ is $3\\,640.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060177 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059670 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema on Transformed Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "132ada2d-225c-55ef-ad34-507a30f3f6af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284958} {:answer/id #uuid "874eb524-3ed8-5726-b354-81594d6ba038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286028} {:answer/id #uuid "8da77226-b79f-5aa1-9fbb-eff1ddcce302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,8)." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286083} {:answer/id #uuid "de9a3fa5-07d3-5f60-9264-81029b284219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286813} {:answer/id #uuid "e77beded-c213-529c-9af4-9172511b2a92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,8)." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "132ada2d-225c-55ef-ad34-507a30f3f6af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284958} :answer-field/id #uuid "e490dbf9-e5ff-5dfa-a559-074583566e32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c0bb866-f5d6-5c26-9148-409419fa5279" :question/math-academy-id "q-186866" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f4da78b7a12ed475b64c1f824e8cecdf36657c6d174f0db770219fd8dd6c0801.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of $y=-2f(x+1)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=-2f(x+1)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $1$ unit to the left, then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, and finally\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global **maximum** of $y=f(x)$ to the global **minimum** of $y=-2f(x+1).$ So, we need to find the image of $(-1,4),$ the global maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-2f(x+1),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-1,4).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1-1,4)=(-2,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $2$ along the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=2f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2,2\\cdot 4)=(-2,8).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we flip the result vertically across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-2f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,8)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2,-1\\cdot 8)=(-2,-8).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=-2f(x+1)$ is $(-2,-8).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401798 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096784 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7578c48-cc9e-5170-8ffe-5ebee6580677" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Decimals in One Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c397f249-716c-58ab-a9bf-91bfc3f7d868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=0.5,\\,b=-1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401800} {:answer/id #uuid "2dec0c3e-7449-5041-96b3-12d8ef936e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=1.5,\\,b=2.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401801} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc082a1-ed8d-5fbd-b12d-2a2df6063e77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=1.2,\\,b=1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401802} {:answer/id #uuid "3da6187f-1bd3-5425-a6fa-9cc0e764d299" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-1.2,\\,b=0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401803} {:answer/id #uuid "ba8550aa-5225-5217-b55a-8b4d6c0d09f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=0.2,\\,b=1.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cc082a1-ed8d-5fbd-b12d-2a2df6063e77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=1.2,\\,b=1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401802} :answer-field/id #uuid "4994c6c6-b044-5fa6-ae9d-9a350ba14b9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8748ba37-2d9a-5451-b497-621aa22378b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-18688" :question/problem "Find the solution of the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}b=2a-1 \\\\ 1.5a+0.5b=2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply the second equation by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}b=2a-1 &  \\\\ 1.5a+0.5b=2.5 & |\\,\\times 10\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}b=2a-1 \\\\ 15a+5b=25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=2a-1\n$$\n\nNow, to find the value of $a,$ we can substitute $b=2a-1$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15a+5(2a-1) & =25 \\\\ 15a+10a-5 & =25 \\\\ 25a & =30 \\\\ a & =\\frac{30}{25} \\\\ a & =1.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $a=1.2$ into the  first equation to get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =2(1.2)-1 \\\\  & =2.4-1 \\\\  & =1.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $a=1.2$ and $b=1.4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287900 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc62d8df-450f-5f7a-b647-b53c051f8339" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Decimals in Both Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e87017a-3d71-526f-882c-4c2dfef523bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285378} {:answer/id #uuid "779e6eca-f602-5727-b029-1c18d87c1280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285898} {:answer/id #uuid "77d04a2c-eb10-5431-830c-d23e900376e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285903} {:answer/id #uuid "8a09092a-f7a8-583a-ab31-352d4e305731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286049} {:answer/id #uuid "d485a384-181e-5a8e-9b1f-2a7c89a1bcb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77d04a2c-eb10-5431-830c-d23e900376e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285903} :answer-field/id #uuid "8315d25a-73c2-509a-9efb-209d1dae20b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "afe3b13e-ab14-5340-83f7-304f398b7445" :question/math-academy-id "q-18720" :question/problem "Let $(p,q)$ be the solution of the following system. What is the value of $p+2q?$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0.36p+1.2q=0.36 \\\\ -1.9p-7.6q=3.8\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply the first equation by $100$ and the second equation by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0.36p+1.2q=0.36 & |\\times 100 \\\\ -1.9p-7.6q=3.8 & |\\times 10\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}36p+120q=36 \\\\ -19p-76q=38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can solve for $p$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-19p-76q & =38 \\\\ -19p & =76q+38 \\\\ p & =\\frac{76q+38}{-19} \\\\ p & =-4q-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the value of $q,$ we can substitute $p=-4q-2$ into the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36p+120q & =36 \\\\ 36(-4q-2)+120q & =36 \\\\ -144q-72+120q & =36 \\\\ -24q & =108 \\\\ q & =-\\frac{108}{24} \\\\ q & =-4.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $q=-4.5$ into the equation $p=-4q-2$ to get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =-4q-2 \\\\ p & =-4(-4.5)-2 \\\\ p & =18-2 \\\\ p & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=16$ and $q=-4.5.$ So, \n\n$$\np+2q=16+2(-4.5)=16-9=7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105729 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83423c03-d314-5f78-9acf-58cbb6f7774b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe61b3a1-9e6d-55b4-b6b7-27c3bed2c392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨ - \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{74}}, \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{74}}, \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{74}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102483} {:answer/id #uuid "186ff4db-a684-5234-a9fe-d4445b22cdd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{74}}, \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{74}}, \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{74}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102484} {:answer/id #uuid "22ad60e1-fb01-562a-88b8-08b42c1e9884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨ - \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{77}},-\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{77}},-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{77}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102485} {:answer/id #uuid "28b4bc21-f68e-5747-965e-a89aa5afb118" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{77}}, \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{77}}, \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{77}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102486} {:answer/id #uuid "49474f4b-2912-595f-95b0-9e42acee4bb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨ - \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{77}}, \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{77}}, \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{77}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102487}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9edea1ff-b532-59c5-b499-57648c90c425" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102482} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f99cbb1-2bbb-59be-947b-044557644091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{77}},\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{77}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{77}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390348} {:answer/id #uuid "1a526c30-a852-56c6-b14c-c2025f540361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{77}},-\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{77}},-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{77}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390349} {:answer/id #uuid "ab385b24-8ef8-5f1a-bd08-498b2099ce33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{74}},\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{74}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{74}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390350} {:answer/id #uuid "585a8c8c-14a7-54a3-b24f-e4f0fbf0ee6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{77}},\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{77}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{77}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390351} {:answer/id #uuid "353305b2-1bf3-530e-9fe4-e18cf98ffcba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{74}},\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{74}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{74}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f99cbb1-2bbb-59be-947b-044557644091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{77}},\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{77}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{77}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390348} :answer-field/id #uuid "13eb807a-8732-502e-90a4-aea8f6fe280d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "46dce84e-4f15-50e3-8511-5dd62cf1728a" :question/math-academy-id "q-18975" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨-2,8,3⟩.$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a}{|\\,a\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\,| & =\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{8}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+64+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{77}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $a$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{a}{|\\,a\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{77}}a \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{77}}\\cdot ⟨-2,8,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{77}},\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{77}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{77}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287913 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105730 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a087e7bc-4e74-584d-af3b-bf6bee4b75ea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Their Components"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0daa6c22-b6f3-51d9-a6de-9fda6e8596b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{85.82}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284905} {:answer/id #uuid "1abecb7e-2e0a-5388-81ae-f79fa8d93b52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{79.76}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285026} {:answer/id #uuid "2ac816ad-f66f-5fc9-a2a3-07c82119d299" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{82.28}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285162} {:answer/id #uuid "69f3be6b-4783-52f4-84fe-9b95d561ffc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{94.32}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285757} {:answer/id #uuid "ee1020da-a591-5782-8a4a-25069e02b21f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{74.66}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0daa6c22-b6f3-51d9-a6de-9fda6e8596b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{85.82}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284905} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbf80208-e230-50e2-a705-9a72d129b4fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "213ad054-3594-5fcc-a936-bdc01a05c42f" :question/math-academy-id "q-19036" :question/problem "Find the angle between $a=⟨4,-3,2⟩$ and $b=⟨3,4,1⟩.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Rearranging the dot product formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}\\cdot \\sqrt{26}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{754}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{754}})≈{85.82}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287915 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105753 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a418d9aa-d74d-58b1-a4c9-819f63593d50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given the Value of Their Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ad90516-6de3-5322-9799-56d50aedcba7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{104.5}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284878} {:answer/id #uuid "2e4ce063-2662-501f-a7d4-6cffbba3f19a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{110.8}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285201} {:answer/id #uuid "38a6c708-fe56-5283-a85a-91fad1eaa1c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{92.3}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285317} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba26421-66e7-5b20-882a-624c20d91ba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{84.7}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285628} {:answer/id #uuid "85ac83f4-5a71-50ed-ae67-c0b78cdc3044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{132.4}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ad90516-6de3-5322-9799-56d50aedcba7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{104.5}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284878} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d084ddf-6dab-583a-a6fe-289d9ce77544" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "024f1eea-1cf9-519a-baef-cf481604490c" :question/math-academy-id "q-19038" :question/problem "Given that $p\\cdot q=-5$, find the angle between $p=⟨4t,-2,t⟩$ and $q=⟨1,3,-3⟩.$ Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." :question/worked-solution "First, since $p\\cdot q=-5$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =-5 \\\\ 4t\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 3+t\\cdot (-3) & =-5 \\\\ 4t-6-3t & =-5 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =⟨4(1),-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,-2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $p$ and $q$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{p\\cdot q}{|\\,p\\,||\\,q\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{p}_{x}^{2}+{p}_{y}^{2}+{p}_{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{21}\\cdot \\sqrt{19}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{399}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTHerefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{399}})≈{104.5}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7995b377-1de4-5126-aa29-8cc30b4918f8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent at a Point of a Function Involving Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04d15acc-1672-5ec4-bb78-f3474884b897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}-5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284830} {:answer/id #uuid "48fabaf5-be8a-589c-8cf0-074c455a9240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}-10\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285458} {:answer/id #uuid "63f73e2e-4004-5fc8-9825-f8337f6c2b07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}-10\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285697} {:answer/id #uuid "766bb2aa-4af5-5e09-aed3-a777505c1a5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}+10\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285891} {:answer/id #uuid "e40e7c86-1ed2-5d5e-9940-61e65ceaec5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}-10\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e40e7c86-1ed2-5d5e-9940-61e65ceaec5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}-10\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286873} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8570317-ecab-5ed9-a868-188d2c2ead8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9daa906-697e-5ad9-9a96-96e98679f403" :question/math-academy-id "q-1904" :question/problem "Find the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ if $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+5csc⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative using the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{d}{dx}(-{x}^{2}+5csc⁡x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-x)+5(-csc⁡xcot⁡x) \\\\  & =-2x-5csc⁡xcot⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into the derivative and get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =-2(\\frac{\\pi }{6})-5csc⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})cot⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}-5\\cdot 2\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}-10\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287920 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115400 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "905d1f6a-72aa-5984-8c7d-920497fd58e4" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Inverse Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions Using the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08847216-8695-5c58-9c55-4efd9ea37cd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3{x}^{2}+2)arccotx-\\frac{{x}^{3}+2x}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284855} {:answer/id #uuid "713ca5fb-8006-5933-96ce-17ac96219558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3{x}^{2}+2)arccotx-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285826} {:answer/id #uuid "75c3ab8f-18e2-59e5-b409-f23279f422cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "arccotx-\\frac{({x}^{3}+2x)(3{x}^{2}+2)}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285882} {:answer/id #uuid "a0bb6a0e-a3e1-53de-bf1c-a588c28c1165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3{x}^{2}+2)-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286254} {:answer/id #uuid "a3628ab9-5c34-5c92-bef6-1314d9e06e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "arccotx-\\frac{{x}^{3}+2x}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08847216-8695-5c58-9c55-4efd9ea37cd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3{x}^{2}+2)arccotx-\\frac{{x}^{3}+2x}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284855} :answer-field/id #uuid "751d5f7c-e24c-5495-bd5f-426fdee41841" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd1d008a-84eb-52df-bb09-d41421bc5f0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-19065" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)=({x}^{3}+2x)arccotx.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of $arccot$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arccotx)=-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule and the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(({x}^{3}+2x)arccotx) \\\\  & =arccotx\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}+2x)+({x}^{3}+2x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arccotx) \\\\  & =arccotx\\cdot (3{x}^{2}+2)+({x}^{3}+2x)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}+2)arccotx-\\frac{{x}^{3}+2x}{{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287921 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064367 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5582e8b-3870-5a32-b15e-62b8f8306ce6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Inverse Trigonometric Functions Using the Product or Quotient Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1618e03f-9620-5bd5-85cd-1b7993323dd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2xarctan({x}^{3})}{{x}^{6}+1}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}{1+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284987} {:answer/id #uuid "2be26547-4c09-5374-8bce-e8a7c5344cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2xarctan({x}^{3})}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}{1+{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285170} {:answer/id #uuid "35fc1512-0fee-559d-9989-9672ff8d1d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285285} {:answer/id #uuid "5c628087-cf37-51b1-ac2c-fb127ba0396b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285634} {:answer/id #uuid "c234a623-5300-5076-898f-6693292506e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{arctan({x}^{3})}{{x}^{2}+1}-\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}{1+{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2be26547-4c09-5374-8bce-e8a7c5344cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2xarctan({x}^{3})}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}{1+{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285170} :answer-field/id #uuid "418f8f05-ac62-5559-a3d3-33900d47dceb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9a202483-d8b8-51e4-982b-f5e54acb06af" :question/math-academy-id "q-19067" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)arctan({x}^{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the product rule, using the formula for the derivative of $arctanx$ and applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =arctan({x}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1))+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arctan({x}^{3})) \\\\  & =arctan({x}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{1+({x}^{3})^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =arctan({x}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot 2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{1+({x}^{3})^{2}}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xarctan({x}^{3})}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}{1+{x}^{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064170 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67f41769-a0ff-5ecc-8b05-5890a7ac9fe3" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using Arcsecant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46152cad-db26-5489-9885-4872107442fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11arcsecx+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285429} {:answer/id #uuid "729aed06-b282-521d-acb9-4668cd5b0175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "arcsec(11x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285842} {:answer/id #uuid "938eb1ee-7ab4-5c5a-9a1e-41f61385c5ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "arccsc(11x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286131} {:answer/id #uuid "bacc3b3a-e948-590d-a0b3-c31bb9639de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "arcsecx+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286488} {:answer/id #uuid "cf0db5b1-a31d-566b-9b38-cd041089c3ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11arccscx+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46152cad-db26-5489-9885-4872107442fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11arcsecx+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285429} :answer-field/id #uuid "01c584f0-c6ff-5109-ba98-24519ff2f970" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5ca0aa8-06db-5c6f-bb07-38d950f613d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-19100" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{11}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{arcsec}\\,x.\n$$\n\nThen we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{11}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =11\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =11arcsecx+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064185 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e6b5d85-0b53-580f-9520-6c33463231d5" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using Inverse Trigonometric Functions and Some Manipulations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02d1fed6-2003-5f27-aae5-10563638a94a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3x-3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284812} {:answer/id #uuid "4a505557-0c7c-5346-91d3-f43699946a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{arccos}\\,x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285476} {:answer/id #uuid "857d016b-947d-51cc-a94f-504ff730be03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286014} {:answer/id #uuid "95a187aa-f975-58a0-836c-3c543a56e0fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{arctan}\\,x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286147} {:answer/id #uuid "ea53131b-47dd-5ccd-bab6-9d3aa84dd5a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+3\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "857d016b-947d-51cc-a94f-504ff730be03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286014} :answer-field/id #uuid "18a42158-518b-5329-b494-893d421c1e55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a43a9d4-962c-592e-be6c-4c9a905c5e08" :question/math-academy-id "q-19102" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "This integral doesn't look like one of the basic ones. However, we can manipulate the numerator a bit  to make it look more like the denominator, which reveals a more familiar expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1} & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}+3-3}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}^{2}+1)-3}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}^{2}+1)}{{x}^{2}+1}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cancel{({x}^{2}+1)}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}+1}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =3-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemembering that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwe can now calculate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (3-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x-3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105731 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7b1b8dc-bb50-59b4-8716-bdd6fbeada4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Three Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ea29480-cac0-5cc3-8047-adc941fa21a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{120}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285655} {:answer/id #uuid "75622db7-e002-5c1b-9372-fa25bdc45120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285873} {:answer/id #uuid "b204700e-83e0-5a79-9f25-c452d7d023d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286416} {:answer/id #uuid "b826d348-d7ac-59b4-9416-af96b6d8c91e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286469} {:answer/id #uuid "fc260a7d-4cf6-56c4-8c1c-5138b1ec36db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75622db7-e002-5c1b-9372-fa25bdc45120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285873} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bd5b7c8-b33e-53f5-923e-d59cd403ae02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6f80f82-94f9-5bde-ac4d-44929ef66109" :question/math-academy-id "q-19110" :question/problem "Find $∠QPR$ given the points $P(0,4,5)$, $Q(1,5,4)$, and $R(2,3,6).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨0,4,5⟩,\\,q=⟨1,5,4⟩,\\,r=⟨2,3,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](images/30/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨1,5,4⟩-⟨0,4,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨2,3,6⟩-⟨0,4,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)={90}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401805 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115191 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9699341b-c4e2-547f-8d98-bcda30567272" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a34f86b-faa2-5844-9680-3f6951dc4ecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{4}{csc}^{2}⁡x+8{x}^{3}cot⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401807} {:answer/id #uuid "63214331-a811-5bf2-8cb7-4da5c8c1d678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{4}{cot}^{2}⁡x-8{x}^{3}csc⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401808} {:answer/id #uuid "2285f6af-f131-5a62-954e-164127014717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{4}{csc}^{2}⁡x-8{x}^{3}cot⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401809} {:answer/id #uuid "5a35c415-50b4-5005-8173-6121abbd643e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}csc⁡x-8{x}^{3}cot⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401810} {:answer/id #uuid "cb8e226f-a28c-5b1a-b7df-6f312bfd06a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{4}{csc}^{2}⁡x+8{x}^{3}tan⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2285f6af-f131-5a62-954e-164127014717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{4}{csc}^{2}⁡x-8{x}^{3}cot⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401809} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7aeb284-4f80-510f-8935-07a7e23c626b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee4e083b-fcc9-5eb5-9a27-ac52f9a97afd" :question/math-academy-id "q-1913" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ if $y=-2{x}^{4}cot⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "We calculate the derivative using the product rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{x}^{4}cot⁡x) \\\\  & =(-8{x}^{3})(cot⁡x)+(-2{x}^{4})(-{csc}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =-8{x}^{3}cot⁡x+2{x}^{4}{csc}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287939 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105701 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c9317eb-d1ce-5f42-b69b-a37e643b865b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Projection Using Angle and Magnitude"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51f1a168-e459-5855-9f32-c7f995528292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\sqrt{2}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285543} {:answer/id #uuid "9d0ca20c-6282-53b7-a446-f76828bdd080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18\\sqrt{2}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286224} {:answer/id #uuid "b1302f72-3e46-5025-ab0e-97b643675a59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9\\sqrt{2}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286407} {:answer/id #uuid "c07b1571-e4fd-5a58-a381-73bd15f01a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\sqrt{2}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286548} {:answer/id #uuid "cb5ff959-29d9-5163-867c-ef6c1827374c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{2}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb5ff959-29d9-5163-867c-ef6c1827374c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{2}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286643} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c576604-9cab-5f35-9107-e12da3cf2cf2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b19c031-650e-5cdc-af13-3ca3ed0d48bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-19220" :question/problem "Suppose that $|\\,a\\,|=36$, $|\\,b\\,|=6$ and that the angle between the vectors is $\\theta ={135}^{∘}.$ Find the vector projection of $a$ onto $b$ in terms of the vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the scalar projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =36\\operatorname{cos}⁡({135}^{∘}) \\\\  & =36\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-18\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the unit vector that is parallel to $b:$\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{b}{|\\,b\\,|}=\\frac{b}{6}=\\frac{1}{6}b\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the vector projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & ={\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a\\cdot u \\\\  & =(-18\\sqrt{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}b \\\\  & =-3\\sqrt{2}b\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064370 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1ec1379-ce50-53c7-b04d-3380676ebeb9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at a Particular Moment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e41ae0ea-e83b-581f-a124-c29c127545ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=9.46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401814} {:answer/id #uuid "2ddc81bc-6b74-5b86-9aa6-68b7985e21ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=4.94" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401815} {:answer/id #uuid "4c2d52e0-5f90-57ca-a8a9-c0f4aaadbdfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=10.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401816} {:answer/id #uuid "e34561e5-c74f-5726-99b2-cd744a8924ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=9.24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401817} {:answer/id #uuid "eea394a2-6e5a-5394-a910-d19a50a2530e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=8.36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e41ae0ea-e83b-581f-a124-c29c127545ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=9.46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401814} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab640647-5232-553a-be5a-08e8539221b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59ea1544-758d-5928-bf3f-4995dd8b61f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-19299" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=4{e}^{-t}+3{t}^{2}-2t+1,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity at time $t=2.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{e}^{-t}+3{t}^{2}-2t+1) \\\\  & =-4{e}^{-t}+6t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the velocity at $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the velocity function above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =-4{e}^{-2}+6\\cdot 2-2 \\\\  & =-4{e}^{-2}+10 \\\\  & =9.458\\,658… \\\\  & ≈9.46\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287951 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092261 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4bfd24-dafd-57b3-940a-154a6e0acf3d" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Exponential Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bb72661-686b-55b7-acd2-2a3bd4990839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\cdot {2}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284881} {:answer/id #uuid "0fa45466-3a87-5294-bb09-177ec594bc1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284925} {:answer/id #uuid "7da55036-6972-52f3-847b-a31f95df7852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285944} {:answer/id #uuid "968393cd-2595-5198-a30d-859a805cd56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286156} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d61fb9-24f0-590c-861d-b0dc09f76a1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "968393cd-2595-5198-a30d-859a805cd56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286156} :answer-field/id #uuid "05333763-722c-5f4d-b56e-21e02dec7970" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15807573-b299-5e98-85b6-db47d2fbe91c" :question/math-academy-id "q-1952" :question/problem "Given that $y={2}^{9x},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={2}^{u},\\,u=9x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=9.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{u} \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{9x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287953 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100950 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a180dc90-c6b9-577e-9eef-ed60ecafe4e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to an Equation Containing a Transformed Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21a58fd9-1eec-5255-a842-d9b344d5a32b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285089} {:answer/id #uuid "244c711b-020d-50ea-a3c7-e3f53022f59e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285105} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce9febb-24a9-5950-8f46-fb621aa09f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285362} {:answer/id #uuid "9ab55399-eaab-5bf2-b31d-0cc923874c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286205} {:answer/id #uuid "a252d0dc-972c-57ed-b476-d063056a314b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a252d0dc-972c-57ed-b476-d063056a314b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286275} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad78ce79-2c39-5ac5-96ff-eade1681f9f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00abbf43-3e03-55ab-b7aa-2f9087193808" :question/math-academy-id "q-19756" :question/problem "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=1\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=\\pi a+\\pi n\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{3\\pi }{4} & =0+2\\pi n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}+\\pi n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-\\frac{3}{8}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233619 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179660 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "644b7f09-7f66-50f2-8eba-ef6e009429d0" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Variable Expression Containing a Quotient With Many Factorials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22766496-0abf-50a1-80d1-5c3aa17a5c49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n-8)(n+8)(n+9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285095} {:answer/id #uuid "2decc5e8-9169-5cef-a48f-c52612fb5bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n+8)(n+9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285195} {:answer/id #uuid "68bba672-b656-58bd-ab7d-b6d8e1d686e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n+7)(n+8)(n+9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285750} {:answer/id #uuid "96c58f78-9e89-5c29-947e-3e0a3eea9a98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(n+8)(n+9)}{n-9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286158} {:answer/id #uuid "bd10a16d-830d-53c9-ba1c-682004537a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n+9}{n-9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22766496-0abf-50a1-80d1-5c3aa17a5c49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n-8)(n+8)(n+9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285095} :answer-field/id #uuid "12acb7ca-836f-551c-8db4-207dc9c189b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f3ef0665-6f2a-5dd9-af17-7da892c3b332" :question/math-academy-id "q-19882" :question/problem "Given that $n\\ge 9$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(n-8)!\\,(n+9)!}{(n-9)!\\,(n+7)!}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n+9)! & =(n+9)(n+8)(n+7)! \\\\ (n-8)! & =(n-8)(n-9)!\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can cancel the $(n+7)!$ and $(n-9)!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(n-8)!\\,(n+9)!}{(n-9)!\\,(n+7)!} & =\\frac{[(n-8)\\cdot (n-9)!]\\cdot [(n+9)\\cdot (n+8)\\cdot (n+7)!]}{(n-9)!\\cdot (n+7)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n-8)\\cdot \\cancel{(n-9)!}\\cdot (n+9)\\cdot (n+8)\\cdot \\cancel{(n+7)!}}{\\cancel{(n-9)!}\\cdot \\cancel{(n+7)!}} \\\\  & =(n-8)\\cdot (n+8)\\cdot (n+9) \\\\  & =(n-8)(n+8)(n+9)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287964 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179660 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "644b7f09-7f66-50f2-8eba-ef6e009429d0" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Variable Expression Containing a Quotient With Many Factorials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cdf387f-3a0b-57c6-89ff-676228ded636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2m}{m+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284894} {:answer/id #uuid "1b734ea2-a219-577a-8568-20df8a2e2919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{m+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285031} {:answer/id #uuid "1e6ad601-4800-552a-b12e-a5b40856f5ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2m}{m+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285058} {:answer/id #uuid "4334150f-17b3-522c-8a54-1ba9d10b279e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285408} {:answer/id #uuid "c780a0ee-f288-5cb4-974a-df2c2b28d6ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{m}{m+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cdf387f-3a0b-57c6-89ff-676228ded636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2m}{m+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284894} :answer-field/id #uuid "30b22a75-1b58-5fe1-bc2a-8eed1972c5c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae781a4d-b2cf-5568-b0a5-f23d5dc664e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-19887" :question/problem "Given that $m\\ge 1$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(2m)!\\,(m+4)!}{(m+5)!\\,(2m-1)!}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(m+5)! & =(m+5)(m+4)! \\\\ 2m! & =2m(2m-1)!\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can cancel the $(m+4)!$ and $(2m-1)!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(2m)!\\,(m+4)!}{(m+5)!\\,(2m-1)!} & =\\frac{(2m)\\,\\cdot (2m-1)!\\cdot (m+4)!}{(m+5)\\cdot (m+4)!\\cdot (2m-1)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2m)\\,\\cdot \\cancel{(2m-1)!}\\cdot \\cancel{(m+4)!}}{(m+5)\\cdot \\cancel{(m+4)!}\\cdot \\cancel{(2m-1)!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2m}{m+5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e49e2595-62a1-595a-8df9-99541bccbdf5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Permutations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "110abfe8-256f-586f-8c14-cdbacbf98aa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284936} {:answer/id #uuid "6a8cbe92-7d56-57d4-ac77-8dcc36070b12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285763} {:answer/id #uuid "75559a19-2283-512d-bed1-5bd115893a57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285871} {:answer/id #uuid "7c34d0e6-2814-5d2f-be61-88b870668d34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285933} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f7b5bb-d5a9-5ca5-9642-1dbe2e08c1b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1f7b5bb-d5a9-5ca5-9642-1dbe2e08c1b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286271} :answer-field/id #uuid "29d5b268-f0c4-5b8e-8047-2fa1728600f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4641263-8717-5c07-a2d0-51003a352849" :question/math-academy-id "q-19899" :question/problem "Michael goes to a restaurant and orders a sandwich, a salad, and a muffin. In how many different orders can he decide to eat his food?" :question/worked-solution "There are $3$ foods, so there are $3!$ ways of ordering them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3! & =3\\times 2\\times 1 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are 6 possible arrangements."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26666440-d376-5cb6-b922-9850e5c42eb3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "471b8112-fe03-5559-8fd9-7d262e3aacb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285445} {:answer/id #uuid "51beac23-bf68-50c9-952d-653cee2aa6dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285540} {:answer/id #uuid "71ac35f1-a93a-56ba-81c5-2d2d400444ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285834} {:answer/id #uuid "82f17c2a-2ee4-5365-bf0f-241c55acae58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285989} {:answer/id #uuid "aa74e9c4-0cc5-5f42-a6c7-ce6192d80edf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51beac23-bf68-50c9-952d-653cee2aa6dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285540} :answer-field/id #uuid "8be7a502-2728-5180-92ba-ee5a5a51b7d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fe360508-b564-5f2e-8656-ff74569f459b" :question/math-academy-id "q-20214" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3=0$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3 & =0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](images/6b/6bc5aed39e6421b8ff2416edb75dc888c8f38ae14f99b3d6e28d8e4c9302908f.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi $ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}-2\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\pm \\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\cdot (-\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{25{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064179 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e617f241-254b-5f70-9937-5883b6f28c4f" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Secant-Tangent and Cosecant-Cotangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16d4843e-1503-5b14-9ef4-6d9bd866b768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284994} {:answer/id #uuid "5f513476-ecb1-5481-9bb0-8cd729eba6f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "csc⁡xcot⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285660} {:answer/id #uuid "c159be5b-9ba9-50ec-90b5-6beba2ba516f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "csc⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286558} {:answer/id #uuid "dbdb10ca-14c4-5f21-a345-f92c3250e22c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-sec⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286788} {:answer/id #uuid "f5a909e4-a442-5f93-bb1c-8af5b5eeb86f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-csc⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbdb10ca-14c4-5f21-a345-f92c3250e22c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-sec⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286788} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5759735-03db-58c8-afc1-42a56314fd9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8eefdd68-c49a-5b6b-9578-f46e438f3aac" :question/math-academy-id "q-2038" :question/problem "$\\int (-sec⁡\\theta tan⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta =$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int sec⁡\\theta tan⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta =sec⁡\\theta +C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int (-sec⁡\\theta tan⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta  & =-\\int sec⁡\\theta tan⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-(sec⁡\\theta )+C \\\\  & =-sec⁡\\theta +C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395776 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083240 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8922ab07-5ac7-5f85-bfee-c30de7f4024a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05fd7882-e12b-5d1e-bda2-674211a5aad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395778} {:answer/id #uuid "61e0f80f-69b1-578c-857a-82bc0682835a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395779} {:answer/id #uuid "26a77c96-4bef-5e82-8889-1c4a9ce7d523" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395780} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9b090b-3cbc-511c-95ab-35fc8c1fc057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395781} {:answer/id #uuid "d1c50146-0d7c-501d-bf37-4d8d838c3cef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c9b090b-3cbc-511c-95ab-35fc8c1fc057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395781} :answer-field/id #uuid "56d7a736-a513-51fb-b585-d99caa2d0531" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45b1f64f-9248-57a2-a310-050eb4c80ee5" :question/math-academy-id "q-20849" :question/problem "![](images/c9/c9371d5e2bb921b4f5e58423bbce492f2b555122ee42bd861040b57d01070353.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=2(t+1),\\,y=4{t}^{2},\\,-1\\le t<1,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{8t}{2}=4t.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=0$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =4(0) \\\\  & =0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401840 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083240 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8922ab07-5ac7-5f85-bfee-c30de7f4024a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e5ec4eb-fb50-5d50-87c7-e15a073c23f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401842} {:answer/id #uuid "7b15bc55-e6d8-5c21-b55e-568e588591b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401843} {:answer/id #uuid "2c8c26cd-3204-5045-8920-160928c0068e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401844} {:answer/id #uuid "7828179c-427e-5c33-a7ae-bd0edbe05cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401845} {:answer/id #uuid "db0ee922-0122-505b-abc7-21b2699ea220" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e5ec4eb-fb50-5d50-87c7-e15a073c23f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401842} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ec6f4c2-cef2-5136-bda0-046075ea56a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a11f695c-004e-57f4-b1f5-cd09434f69e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-20851" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=2\\sqrt{t},\\,y=12t+1,\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=12.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{12}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}}=12\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =12\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =12\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390402 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105894 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of an Object Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd55c037-00e6-5219-914e-9a5a9bd7534e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e667849ed8a214e7d17871b0f96f73f83c59eec2677bfbaeaab589e04cc89e61.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390404} {:answer/id #uuid "f174cb91-e1ee-5d6c-90ef-6029cbaebf29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/52/52266ff97300b29c85ac14f5b60b308d9e8a0e2fe3a01e8e0431b643e6a28922.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390405} {:answer/id #uuid "071cdea1-0dac-5f25-b6f4-63481ba1e33c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1a/1a027e19617c0c62fd8f5247c6d1bbac894f9522ec9e90aec5b7ed05b3996b37.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390406} {:answer/id #uuid "dd9aba44-7003-5d32-9bce-752796690c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ca/cac3c2b4fb6191aa9a523b445d8533774f438d24556ea79b8e67a4d6c3867526.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390407} {:answer/id #uuid "38ce622d-f9f2-50a8-99cd-404657278c3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f7/f7033ca262624a9723186563638f5ff006d4174a82349be0812ac3de4c25af5e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f174cb91-e1ee-5d6c-90ef-6029cbaebf29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/52/52266ff97300b29c85ac14f5b60b308d9e8a0e2fe3a01e8e0431b643e6a28922.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390405} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cfcca37-0c5f-5e1f-94b7-02dbf414d41e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7e170ff-3ef3-51ef-bb76-d32ad791f4a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-21058" :question/problem "![](images/fe/fee79ec0b214a4ba87400b4b8c9dab2c623878d067821c572b26ae241625c48a.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 10 & 15 & 25\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(2,10)$, $(3,15)$, and  $(5,25).$  Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](images/bf/bf5987fde11416780a6a599e3952db7319e1c0e859e2439b7df70c5659bc6839.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290269 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105894 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of an Object Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b93f461-1c7c-56fa-9e47-cf342c2182e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/40/40f352980b38be63b071b160953eff23a98996dce58c5f616e10958a8a70182e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288119} {:answer/id #uuid "6d878e48-5182-54e0-9a21-7580d9175240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ee/ee4a96b2b1106fbd1a5ec222c3f7f2a5e5a9e84c9bfe539aeb29a1918943aef9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288798} {:answer/id #uuid "84bcd4c8-95e4-5ba2-812e-8d15253507a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/dc/dc5ad781e838e597706600633701871006cd59fd3db60344cf34aaddb4200da8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288924} {:answer/id #uuid "bae133f8-955a-587b-8e5f-630c8d7aa03e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/92/92beb71e7d09cba01f2eeb82a60ca3b0b3929064e4f16da3af104db8c5eaa8df.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289265} {:answer/id #uuid "be768ba3-bb22-5082-9403-9396a068e5ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b4/b4d2150d92ff14a654fb7079a17d7bd75abe721c0d37d4075d5a92635bafc170.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d878e48-5182-54e0-9a21-7580d9175240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ee/ee4a96b2b1106fbd1a5ec222c3f7f2a5e5a9e84c9bfe539aeb29a1918943aef9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288798} :answer-field/id #uuid "9224408d-95cf-5c4a-a8cc-58f66c41fcd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dcfd5567-90cc-5d33-9aaa-36581e272ed2" :question/math-academy-id "q-21063" :question/problem "![](images/65/6501b1a7e9429c786905c11835410111c32ac0337f1ba63bff130d327350316c.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 8 & 10 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(6,3)$, $(8,4)$ and $(10,5).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](images/a9/a91af151d3dc0e48aef0eef58012f6c9f72aaff02aafae2d55fe0714c29c96e3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059670 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema on Transformed Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01b3ee48-738d-5154-93f4-593da455f6f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288038} {:answer/id #uuid "02202242-35fc-558e-b9c0-5e30f23f7048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288042} {:answer/id #uuid "7017f50c-dcb4-516e-bbbc-55369bab2f30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288810} {:answer/id #uuid "c4904f95-4b3c-5515-9fcf-0a99b245182b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289330} {:answer/id #uuid "cbce72d8-a3b3-5305-909c-46fd579eba75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7017f50c-dcb4-516e-bbbc-55369bab2f30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288810} :answer-field/id #uuid "09418823-c229-5178-abcd-3466929f3782" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0430c7a-d01d-554d-86e5-a8ca9cccf773" :question/math-academy-id "q-211462" :question/problem "![](images/80/8000655c1943c60282d8844a2ae965b6415e21962c44309cd68806f04744b806.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global maximum of $y=-f(2x)+3?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=-f(2x)+3$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis,\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis, and finally\n\n- translating it by $3$ units up.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global **minimum** of $y=f(x)$ to the global **maximum** of $y=-f(2x)+3.$ So, we need to find the image of $(2,3),$ the global minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-f(2x)+3,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,3).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph  along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ and get the graph of $y=f(2x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2,3)=(1,3).\n$$\n\n- Next, we flip the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(2x).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,-1\\cdot 3)=(1,-3).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we translate the result by $3$ units up and get the graph of $y=-f(2x)+3.$ After this transformation, the point $(1,-3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,-3+3)=(1,0).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global maximum of $y=-f(2x)+3$ is $(1,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290294 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105703 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84c96375-27d5-5ada-a952-34e09b3e92bf" :knowledge-point/title "Perpendicular Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05f5cec0-bb91-5c28-a043-4592ab711f53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288076} {:answer/id #uuid "5aa80b1b-8d9e-53e5-b4ff-3afd65b4f60b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288664} {:answer/id #uuid "69174e17-b083-51ab-a263-552d37887450" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288772} {:answer/id #uuid "7f12c795-1d8e-5fc7-8db2-9ce3db4be399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288894} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b8268e-e1e9-5fc0-968f-daa50516ca35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5aa80b1b-8d9e-53e5-b4ff-3afd65b4f60b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288664} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dafe033-6f62-51c0-aef0-ed6ade7a0953" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff2e4ed3-b952-5f49-9d70-f52c7f12daca" :question/math-academy-id "q-212457" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨0,1,-5⟩$ and $b=⟨6,x,-2⟩.$ Given that the projection of $a$ onto $b$ is the zero vector, find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals the zero vector only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 6+1\\cdot x+(-5)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =x+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ x+10 & =0 \\\\ x & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390409 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184139 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecbf45e4-67a6-58cd-9655-4a339de64db5" :knowledge-point/title "Inequalities Involving Horizontal and Vertical Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8941003-fece-5a27-9a24-50a0d6f852dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390411} {:answer/id #uuid "0e7b79bb-d162-5d42-938f-17dc6295e499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e7b79bb-d162-5d42-938f-17dc6295e499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390412} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbd21228-5859-522b-b900-855a3b6d470d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390410} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afafe478-2e17-5ff9-82c1-667432a46587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390414} {:answer/id #uuid "00a59daf-ef53-5a0d-8f67-229640ca3ce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390415} {:answer/id #uuid "6c826f8f-5a6b-553e-85ef-a2a25222748f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390416} {:answer/id #uuid "b5af3a45-5715-50e4-a115-829d59895c38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c826f8f-5a6b-553e-85ef-a2a25222748f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390416} :answer-field/id #uuid "478e490a-5240-599f-96de-4e191cdfa2a5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fae4dc0d-5fcc-556e-856f-aa7c560289fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-214907" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cb311261eabb4b5c1b6c4114c92e798f0caf2e326dc90486429d230167f6978b.png)\n\nExpress the shaded region above as an inequality.The shaded region above is described by the inequality\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The border-line is a vertical line passing through the point $(-2,0).$ Therefore, the line has the equation $x=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use. Since the line is dashed, we will use a strict inequality. Since the shaded region is to the *right* of the line, the inequality refers to the $x$-values that are *greater than* those on the line $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\nx>-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184139 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecbf45e4-67a6-58cd-9655-4a339de64db5" :knowledge-point/title "Inequalities Involving Horizontal and Vertical Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "658db8cb-ba47-576d-98bf-392b1c972351" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390420} {:answer/id #uuid "9a199d3b-122e-5520-ab63-cad8960ce2ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390421} {:answer/id #uuid "d68d8126-7623-5eaf-bd8f-5ac5d9474dda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390422} {:answer/id #uuid "823ad81d-b696-522f-ab58-29ea625c84a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d68d8126-7623-5eaf-bd8f-5ac5d9474dda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390422} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a5871fe-f6ee-5364-9ea7-6831196e61f7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390419} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "603761b4-58e0-582d-bb8b-85f71e2feec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390425} {:answer/id #uuid "6e4374b4-87ee-5f43-8d23-b5167780b2a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e4374b4-87ee-5f43-8d23-b5167780b2a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390426} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba292289-f6b9-5d5c-b56b-a6376634f5af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84d30d8f-6033-5842-95cc-601ebb4c6d51" :question/math-academy-id "q-215437" :question/problem "![](images/bb/bb24be727a3bb001e25b5f2c758b1302332aa8b45a51cfd0d4884b2784479e58.png)\n\nExpress the shaded region above as an inequality.\nThe shaded region above is described by the inequality\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The border-line is a vertical line passing through the point $(-1,0).$ Therefore, the line has the equation $x=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use. Since the line is solid, we will use an \"or equal\" inequality. Since the shaded region is to the *left* of the line, the inequality refers to the $x$-values that are *less than or equal to* those on the line $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\nx\\le -1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290323 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110599 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29005c66-9f30-5862-926f-8a09320d404c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Slope-Intercept Form are Perpendicular"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f2f9488-176e-5ee3-ad8e-2db95b1123f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288350} {:answer/id #uuid "3f03d5e0-e5b1-53b3-ab18-0d7e379a24cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288461} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf2b0ef-9634-5c51-86e7-60eaa13bc504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289396} {:answer/id #uuid "cea07821-893d-5fac-a3e9-4d99a591dc1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289410} {:answer/id #uuid "f789d1aa-f223-5771-a67e-fc052240756a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f03d5e0-e5b1-53b3-ab18-0d7e379a24cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288461} :answer-field/id #uuid "aed86ac2-1482-5201-9d78-def088012c7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da3fa1e4-9073-5723-b4d9-3e3d9f56d008" :question/math-academy-id "q-215890" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $y=x+\\frac{1}{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nThe line $y=x+\\frac{1}{2}$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $1.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=-x+9$ has a slope of $-1.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n1\\cdot (-1)=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=-x+9$ is perpendicular to the given line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14370371-529b-5f25-9963-62c0cd9a172e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in One Equation Using Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28be3240-f527-5881-b030-d0e2f6f588f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=9,\\,q=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288304} {:answer/id #uuid "2cd4f05d-ba10-5b34-a545-fb122617cedb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=5,\\,q=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288324} {:answer/id #uuid "3c45f0cb-960d-58d2-8635-289e83d41c5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288448} {:answer/id #uuid "4eeddfa0-2016-5856-83e5-03c446213f2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-3,\\,q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288578} {:answer/id #uuid "c538b0db-744e-5c84-a6a0-48a8617f619c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c538b0db-744e-5c84-a6a0-48a8617f619c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289338} :answer-field/id #uuid "3459c60d-978a-5a3f-861a-110bccb1e94a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "906fc64a-e7c8-5770-89dc-d9c9f6b082d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-216042" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3p+\\frac{4}{5}q=-1 \\\\ 9p-4q=-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above." :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the fractions in the first equation, we must multiply the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $5.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3p+\\frac{4}{5}q=-1 & |\\,\\times 5 \\\\ 9p-4q=-13\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-15p+4q=-5 \\\\ 9p-4q=-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that the $4q$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-4q$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $q$-variable, and allows us to solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-15p+\\cancel{4q} & =-5 \\\\ 9p-\\cancel{4q} & =-13 \\\\ -6p & =-18 \\\\ p & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $p=3$ into the equation $9p-4q=-13$ to solve for $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9p-4q & =-13 \\\\ 9\\cdot 3-4q & =-13 \\\\ 27-4q & =-13 \\\\ -4q & =-40 \\\\ q & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=3,\\,q=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064178 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0cae5c1b-57bd-51f6-a360-db946d25532f" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Sine and Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "346358f5-312e-5c8a-9238-6be633b31e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-sin⁡x-cos⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288379} {:answer/id #uuid "419bbacf-36c2-544f-888a-5bf28372bd28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-sin⁡x+cos⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288478} {:answer/id #uuid "70205983-7a23-51c1-a345-bf2ec479a0bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2sin⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288812} {:answer/id #uuid "ba4296e8-700e-5668-8c90-e562531a6962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sin⁡x+cos⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289261} {:answer/id #uuid "c6a193d2-0ce7-5a28-86c4-834fae7f7271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sin⁡x-cos⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba4296e8-700e-5668-8c90-e562531a6962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sin⁡x+cos⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289261} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c59fbc8-d124-507b-af30-f117e1976ad1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d70818e9-1705-53df-abe3-9e6e72f287c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-2161" :question/problem "$\\int (cos⁡x-sin⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int sin⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-cos⁡x+C, \\\\ \\int cos⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =sin⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (cos⁡x-sin⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int cos⁡x\\,\\text{d}x-\\int sin⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =sin⁡x-(-cos⁡x)+C \\\\  & =sin⁡x+cos⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171915 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8874325-af33-506b-b24d-3a3e387bd88c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Roots of Unity With Certain Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "791706c1-fc72-5123-9e31-3c7ce30854b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{9\\pi \\text{i}/14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288864} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce573f9-48e8-5ac3-aa97-939de73f0108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{11\\pi \\text{i}/14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289090} {:answer/id #uuid "a2fdc674-96ab-5ac8-a0ab-d0809b2008c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6\\pi \\text{i}/7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289120} {:answer/id #uuid "e0df3657-bbfa-5488-8746-ee68814b11da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3\\pi \\text{i}/7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289534} {:answer/id #uuid "f9884108-f3e3-5cb0-b016-a9030f916564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2fdc674-96ab-5ac8-a0ab-d0809b2008c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6\\pi \\text{i}/7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289120} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5eae31a-74ea-5e55-88a7-2b9c77bcd639" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "87c48cd8-6b59-5b2c-8f86-b6fedd6aff90" :question/math-academy-id "q-216706" :question/problem "![](images/89/89d0606f11701cb59685e5ea1540b8ed98ce8cfe371951a675f01a1c6cc5a748.png)\n\nFind the $7$th root of unity highlighted in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0).$\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{7}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/7}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}/7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/34/344d760f2cd08e5e1e1eccc2664e7182d01744ee9b0b15f985c6d7b4e0e5515d.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=3.$ Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{3}={e}^{2(3)\\pi \\text{i}/7}={e}^{6\\pi \\text{i}/7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290345 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171915 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8874325-af33-506b-b24d-3a3e387bd88c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Roots of Unity With Certain Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e3a3ed8-0354-5f4f-89c9-625d38e3ddb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288691} {:answer/id #uuid "9ad99bdc-f0d4-5a04-97c9-680d63f9502a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289070} {:answer/id #uuid "b36ddb2a-bd92-52f9-8cc4-1b618625e41e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289216} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d482cf-08e9-5fe6-be00-11761fcd614f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289235} {:answer/id #uuid "d53245fa-a4f6-5abc-b9a4-2d6a0a3a3109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d53245fa-a4f6-5abc-b9a4-2d6a0a3a3109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289449} :answer-field/id #uuid "f65e506f-26ae-56f1-b87a-7435b1f33099" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7996f9b1-41da-5b9e-ba97-39356ddebf59" :question/math-academy-id "q-216709" :question/problem "![](images/bf/bf78e90c844df2d80ffb3b08476ecd0370ffbbf63050bcabf20265692b2deeb3.png)\n\nFind the root of unity highlighted in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0).$\n\nSince there are $9$ points in our diagram, we need to find a $9$th root of unity.\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{9}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/9}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}/9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/e7/e797056434dc122218bdc501cd9e1d5af93127658daa14f7c38b3bd7be8b877f.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=3.$ Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{3}={e}^{2(3)\\pi \\text{i}/9}={e}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290346 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171915 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8874325-af33-506b-b24d-3a3e387bd88c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Roots of Unity With Certain Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "019f383c-4240-5aa1-8167-9eeec6560832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288036} {:answer/id #uuid "3089eabc-41b3-575d-9707-096e1189e460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288352} {:answer/id #uuid "36b865dc-3f00-5c77-8e65-143b51a1cd23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{16\\pi \\text{i}/9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288398} {:answer/id #uuid "3907c5c9-15e2-5165-983c-58c43d4ca77d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288423} {:answer/id #uuid "a07a07da-8c7f-5d52-a274-e0fe0101c4e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{11\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3907c5c9-15e2-5165-983c-58c43d4ca77d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288423} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b8d3be4-ff98-5294-8a8c-5e871bca47da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76a645d8-58db-587a-a38f-24be87eafa21" :question/math-academy-id "q-216710" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b575a8b3c4830aa48586b457b45f76ca8df49ecf8036df720f66aaddc857bdaf.png)\n\nFind the root of unity highlighted in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0).$\n\nSince there are $6$ points in our diagram, we need to find a $6$th root of unity.\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{6}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/6}={e}^{k\\pi \\text{i}/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/34/34a3bb77a9765cd806a047ad42a7f0719201effdd46659efe7ffd1a94a051910.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=5.$ Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{5}={e}^{(5)\\pi \\text{i}/3}={e}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068722 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b89c50f3-2181-5b61-96cb-386ef88d2035" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Expression Involving a Power Function Multiplied by Its Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d5927e7-3d1a-5589-9ab4-b01b479f66f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x({x}^{2}+1)^{13}}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288882} {:answer/id #uuid "a65798db-1640-5ac0-9550-f0316b86d818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{13}}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289133} {:answer/id #uuid "a71c627c-e9b2-5a11-b21d-b190f3a77006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x({x}^{2}+1)^{13}}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289138} {:answer/id #uuid "d436ea16-40f5-50d5-8944-8947c0a63c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x({x}^{2}+1)^{12}}{12}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289436} {:answer/id #uuid "eb4463e3-17c4-52b2-809c-06d7c4b4e286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{12}}{12}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a65798db-1640-5ac0-9550-f0316b86d818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{13}}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289133} :answer-field/id #uuid "827ea8a6-3f45-58a9-acce-ef8eb4a48299" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4b49482-74ae-5d27-81da-2160ac3cb246" :question/math-academy-id "q-2195" :question/problem "$\\int 2x({x}^{2}+1)^{12}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $2x$ is the derivative of the quantity in the parentheses. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand rewriting our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x({x}^{2}+1)^{12}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({x}^{2}+1)^{12}\\,2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{12}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\int {u}^{12}\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{{u}^{13}}{13}+C\n$$\n\nLastly, we substitute $u={x}^{2}+1$ back in the expression. So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\int 2x({x}^{2}+1)^{12}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{13}}{13}+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b494140f-206a-5317-8079-ff719a253140" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Matrices Are Conformable for Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8292c378-f477-55da-80ea-56bd4135cf7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288910} {:answer/id #uuid "95fa9c35-2493-581e-bec2-06a09917fa48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289035} {:answer/id #uuid "afdc0aa9-1534-5121-892d-7fd982536645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289195} {:answer/id #uuid "eb3234b4-fa2a-5696-8475-0c7cca9ca765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289588} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e33c97-12ab-549d-88a5-9b6b03088a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95fa9c35-2493-581e-bec2-06a09917fa48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289035} :answer-field/id #uuid "e798780e-db3f-5004-a9f5-57c606c3d4b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f63e1f3-652b-58b6-9207-73ea266277c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-22055" :question/problem "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,Y=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Z=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the matrices $X,Y,$ and $Z?$\n\n- The product $XY$ is well defined\n- The product $YX$ is well defined\n- The product $YZ$ is well defined" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $X$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, $Y$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $Z$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\nThere are $2$ columns in $X$ and $2$ rows in $Y.$ Since the number of columns in $X$ equals the number of rows in $Y,$ the matrices $X$ and $Y$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{X}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{2}}}{\\times }_{✓}{Y}_{\\boxed{\\text{2}}\\times 3}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $3$ columns in $Y$ and $2$ rows in $X.$ Since the number of columns in $Y$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $X,$ the matrices $Y$ and $X$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{Y}_{2\\times 3}{\\times }_{\\ne }{X}_{2\\times 2}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $3$ columns in $Y$ and $3$ rows in $Z.$ Since the number of columns in $Y$ equals the number of rows in $Z,$ the matrices $Y$ and $Z$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{Y}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\times }_{✓}{Z}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 3}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nTherefore, statements I and III are true, while statement II is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064177 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "980bb2fb-4bb5-5cbb-aea8-87010493c013" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Sum of Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39f1030c-14e2-59e9-a189-ae72636d7cec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}+\\frac{{5}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288429} {:answer/id #uuid "831fe1aa-d1a9-59de-914e-66c8e45135cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{x}+{2}^{x}ln⁡2+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288914} {:answer/id #uuid "a92a1f84-319a-5ffc-912d-da344601d75d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}}{5}+\\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289153} {:answer/id #uuid "bfec5a66-eb21-510f-ac20-724d2da23f30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{x}+\\frac{x}{ln⁡2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289301} {:answer/id #uuid "f611671a-7317-5307-aefb-d9046cadfb32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{x}+\\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f611671a-7317-5307-aefb-d9046cadfb32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{x}+\\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289655} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b01bf45-11e5-550e-91d5-6d78dcabad9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3b4a92d-a353-507a-becb-bee354a44152" :question/math-academy-id "q-2277" :question/problem "$\\int (5{e}^{x}+{2}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int (5{e}^{x}+{2}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{e}^{x}+\\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bf25f86-6b27-5eaa-b1a1-1104478b290a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Function Given in Implicit Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e268375-538a-538a-80bc-2f086cffdbb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,{2}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288224} {:answer/id #uuid "793b6c32-8350-533d-bf9d-724d40064c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{2}^{y}}{6x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288865} {:answer/id #uuid "a3589f85-19fc-5bd4-ac3b-96bf622c2422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,{2}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289122} {:answer/id #uuid "e0d85bbf-5174-563d-a666-9a94bd4cf6fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{{2}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289532} {:answer/id #uuid "e27c90f5-ce7c-52d7-8488-fe25083eb2d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{1-{2}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e268375-538a-538a-80bc-2f086cffdbb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,{2}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288224} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f58fa9b-a3f6-53aa-a4c8-4680d5988ce9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "623f14ca-5670-5819-81c3-5ed253df18c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-2289" :question/problem "Given that  $y-{2}^{y}=3{x}^{2},$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}y-{2}^{y} & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y-{2}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({2}^{y}) & =6x \\\\ (1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2){2}^{y})(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =6x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,{2}^{y}) & =6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{6x}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,{2}^{y}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290450 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129080 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "021e3349-c54d-55a8-9095-902bba933660" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Pythagorean Theorem to the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "041bb980-2220-5c0f-b904-f70291dc858b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288059} {:answer/id #uuid "1bcd216a-5d96-580d-89a1-1300b816ea9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288211} {:answer/id #uuid "6428772c-31cb-5d11-ad40-b384b65de264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{23}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288738} {:answer/id #uuid "a6ab4c14-8b8f-5d7f-941d-c5430187106a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{23}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289135} {:answer/id #uuid "b4335ae8-54ab-5f8c-98b6-6b1f10f60c80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{7\\sqrt{33}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6428772c-31cb-5d11-ad40-b384b65de264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{23}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288738} :answer-field/id #uuid "94cc4b6d-4396-5dd4-a8e0-bcf717fb1b6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "808fb241-7b41-5e43-96f3-08a62164f95c" :question/math-academy-id "q-23289" :question/problem "![](images/89/8904a5ebafade54a46cee77a2e20cac255c888d257ef1c208b2def32d247f2cd.png)\n\nThe point $P$ is located on the unit circle, as shown above. The $y$-coordinate of $P$ is $\\frac{11}{12}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $P$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=\\frac{11}{12}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{11}{12})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{121}{144}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{121}{144} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{23}{144} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{23}{144}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{23}}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the negative answer since $x$ is negative in the second quadrant. So our final answer is $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{23}}{12}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087890 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe05aa67-e29e-5803-980c-e49ecb5db248" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acceleration of a Particle at a Particular Moment Given Its Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ff8525f-bdb5-59b7-9364-7f3238e455f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288149} {:answer/id #uuid "1b1a00c6-778b-593d-99b5-702f58820750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288203} {:answer/id #uuid "42931aef-7dc3-55d6-82f9-d903adc9adb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288484} {:answer/id #uuid "4c581d2f-fca2-53f4-8e91-541c726d08fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288560} {:answer/id #uuid "ff7bc305-8f2b-5684-a01c-e4bf62ba726c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c581d2f-fca2-53f4-8e91-541c726d08fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288560} :answer-field/id #uuid "b56098b1-fb45-5f85-8ed3-7a8cab96d6c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4551db6-cbed-5a0b-aa5c-4809ad316023" :question/math-academy-id "q-2351" :question/problem "The displacement (in meters) of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)=2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+2t$, where $t$ is measured in seconds.  Find the particle's acceleration at $t=2$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+2t) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-6t+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2}-6t+2) \\\\  & =12t-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=2$, we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(2) & =12(2)-6 \\\\  & =24-6 \\\\  & =18\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration when $t=2$ seconds is $18\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059651 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23637774-8d69-500d-8cb0-5e41a0207542" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions Using an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "277373e3-169a-586e-ad29-c1c31afaae90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288292} {:answer/id #uuid "6a095a67-ddbe-5bf7-9a39-f8782eff2ea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288780} {:answer/id #uuid "94d134d0-8d56-5307-b137-977c53f49ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289029} {:answer/id #uuid "ae3d194e-cae3-5d1d-9ecd-a175924b168f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289186} {:answer/id #uuid "cc7343e7-6e46-504c-b190-873fccc910a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc7343e7-6e46-504c-b190-873fccc910a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289392} :answer-field/id #uuid "78b2f4eb-6782-5d0a-9ec1-3e356d9f2e48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d60ea30f-16e8-5e44-938b-3986249a44fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-23526" :question/problem "$\\frac{1-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=$" :question/worked-solution "Since both fractions are over a common denominator, we can add them. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $ to simplify the expression in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110560 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35fe6748-f040-5318-84e0-f3f76c9c7250" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Cotangent-Cosecant Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "108b997f-3ef8-51bd-8557-0ff04c25f1bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288157} {:answer/id #uuid "7041b720-f438-5081-b848-05a44b0aed85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288814} {:answer/id #uuid "78afce16-dd3c-5a3e-ac4b-6998edd1f35d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288862} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b3fb44-66d6-5d64-93da-9ca291156c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289642} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5e503b-835c-559e-adc2-29bda28d6008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7041b720-f438-5081-b848-05a44b0aed85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288814} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f020196-633d-58ab-b488-e011bb361efe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289936}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2a7de17-dd0e-54fa-9258-ee9fa753362a" :question/math-academy-id "q-23602" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the identity $1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ we rewrite and simplify the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087889 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0cdcbc5-c10b-5dd1-af9a-410d2d44a4c5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acceleration of a Particle Given Its Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03ce4bdc-93af-5a54-9a75-4348de1eca82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288056} {:answer/id #uuid "38003279-169b-5abe-bbd8-5dd8613c632b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288412} {:answer/id #uuid "b5e5544f-7d20-58e7-8ae2-104f0dc53c75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289236} {:answer/id #uuid "c004da3d-3b21-5bf2-8b40-1a7f273f69dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289302} {:answer/id #uuid "c111a5f3-32b9-5d72-a5ee-cf9cbe58a3f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5e5544f-7d20-58e7-8ae2-104f0dc53c75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289236} :answer-field/id #uuid "a41fbea3-85aa-5b15-9e85-ef6d9ffbc9e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "df553b92-e7eb-5bae-8500-5506ac0ebe3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-2361" :question/problem "The velocity $v$ (in $\\text{m/s}$) of a particle at time $t$ (in seconds) is given by $v(t)=4{t}^{2}-16t+15.$ Calculate the velocity of the particle when the acceleration $a$ is equal to $0\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate $v(t)$ to calculate $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2}-16t+15) \\\\  & =8t-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the time when the acceleration is $a=0,$ we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =8t-16 \\\\ 8t & =16 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, to calculate the velocity at time $t=2,$ we substitute this into the equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =4(2)^{2}-16(2)+15 \\\\  & =16-32+15 \\\\  & =-1\\text{ m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity when $a=0\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ is $-1\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110600 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e732f270-1d6f-5ff8-8792-d9b329ea858d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Standard Form are Perpendicular"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b644ab2-4c21-5aeb-9032-26a79415192f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288444} {:answer/id #uuid "540e1587-3f3e-52a8-8424-5abf66ff30e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288610} {:answer/id #uuid "70b1df14-3de9-50cb-8b16-2a2eec5b3954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288816} {:answer/id #uuid "959ae715-31b5-5f44-931c-f40632872d6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289030} {:answer/id #uuid "c2d44e35-06ab-5ae0-8bfd-256c7f07808a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70b1df14-3de9-50cb-8b16-2a2eec5b3954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288816} :answer-field/id #uuid "990087b9-37dc-5d9d-b34d-123762544320" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4eec6d2-1c29-5a4c-9b34-ea67f65dce06" :question/math-academy-id "q-23621" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $x+3y=6?$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $x+3y=6,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3y & =6 \\\\ 3y & =-x+6 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=3x$ has a slope of $3.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot 3=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=3x$ is perpendicular to the given line."}]}
